## 7. EPIC 5 — Borrowing Tab (DhanSathi Udaar)

</aside>

**Epic description:** The Borrowing tab exists to prevent users from accessing predatory informal credit by surfacing regulated alternatives before they reach a moneylender. The Vaani check (“Do you actually need to borrow?”) is the first step, not the loan product listing.

**Business value:** Predatory moneylender prevention is a brand-defining product moment. Users who see Rs. 3,000 interest vs. Rs. 550 interest on a Rs. 5,000 loan have a concrete, calculable reason to stay in the DhanSathi ecosystem.

**Success metrics:**

| Metric | Target |
| --- | --- |
| Borrowing tab open rate | 20%+ of active users within first month |
| Moneylender cost comparison view rate | 70%+ of borrowing tab sessions |
| Formal loan option click-through (PM Mudra application initiation) | 15%+ of comparison views |

---

**No Must Have user story covers the Borrowing tab explicitly. This EPIC is P1 overall. The core stories are derived from the product architecture and the research insight that moneylender prevention is a key user outcome.**

**Functional Requirements for the Borrowing Tab:**

| ID | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| FR-BT-01 | Vaani session must be the default entry point to the Borrowing tab; user must answer one question (“How much do you need and for how long?”) before any loan product is shown |
| FR-BT-02 | System must check user’s existing DhanSathi savings balance first; if sufficient, it must present a “Use your savings” option before showing borrowing products |
| FR-BT-03 | Moneylender cost comparison must be shown for the user’s stated borrowing amount before any formal loan product is listed |
| FR-BT-04 | Comparison card must show: moneylender (informal) total interest cost vs. PM Mudra or Jan Samarth total interest cost for the same amount and same repayment term |
| FR-BT-05 | Only NBFC products with annualised rates below 24% and government-backed loan schemes may appear in the product listing |
| FR-BT-06 | “Apply for PM Mudra” or “Apply on Jan Samarth” must open the official portal within an in-app browser (not redirect to external app) |
| FR-BT-07 | User should be able to bypass the Vaani check via a visible “Skip — show loan options” link, which is always present but secondary |

**Modules:**

**M5.1 — Borrowing Need Assessment Module (Vaani Check)**
Vaani session with 1–3 questions: amount needed, repayment timeline, reason (optional). System checks user’s liquid savings balance first. If savings cover the need, Vaani presents that option first. Loan products displayed only after. User flow: Borrowing tab → Vaani asks “कितने रुपये चाहिए?” → Amount stated → Savings check → If sufficient: “Use savings” option shown → If not: Comparison view.

