## 9. EPIC 7 — Trust, Anti-Scam, and Privacy Layer

</aside>

**Epic description:** Three of four research interviewees showed scam anxiety as a primary blocker to engaging with a new financial product. This epic ensures that trust is built architecturally, not as an afterthought. It covers the anti-scam declaration, Account Aggregator consent management, and data privacy compliance.

**Business value:** Without this epic, the product cannot convert users who have been targeted by financial scams. Suresh (Interview 1) blocks all unknown callers because colleagues were scammed. Truptimayee (Interview 3) only trusts products after personal verification. This epic creates the conditions for trust formation.

**Success metrics:**

| Metric | Target |
| --- | --- |
| Anti-scam screen completion rate (user read and confirmed) | 90%+ |
| Account Aggregator consent revocation rate | Below 5% monthly |
| Security-related support tickets | Zero increase post-launch |

---

### US-10 — Anti-Scam OTP Declaration

**Story:** As a user, I want a clear statement that DhanSathi will never call or message me asking for an OTP, so that I can trust the product isn’t a scam.

**Story Points:** 2 · **Priority:** P1 (Should Have)

**Functional Requirements:**

| ID | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| FR-12-01 | Anti-scam declaration screen must appear before any Account Aggregator consent request, and must not be skippable |
| FR-12-02 | Declaration must state in Hindi and English: “DhanSathi kabhi OTP, password, ya bank details phone ya message par nahi maangega. Agar koi DhanSathi ke naam se maange, woh fraud hai.” |
| FR-12-03 | User must tap “I understand” to proceed; declaration cannot be auto-dismissed |
| FR-12-04 | Declaration must reappear if user’s device shows a new DhanSathi login (new device or 30 days since last shown) |
| FR-12-05 | A “Report a scam call” link must be accessible from the app’s Help section, which opens a pre-filled WhatsApp message to DhanSathi support |

**Additional functional requirements for the Trust and Privacy layer (cross-cutting):**

| ID | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| FR-12-06 | Account Aggregator consent must display exact data scope (which data, for how long, for what purpose) in plain language before any consent is recorded |
| FR-12-07 | Users must be able to revoke AA consent from Settings at any time with a single tap; revocation must trigger data deletion within 24 hours |
| FR-12-08 | No user data (financial or personal) may be shared with B2B2C acquisition partners under any condition; partners receive only aggregate enrollment metrics |
| FR-12-09 | All PII must be encrypted at rest (AES-256) and in transit (TLS 1.2+) |
| FR-12-10 | App must comply with DPDP Act 2023: data minimization, purpose limitation, user right to delete, and privacy notice in Hindi |

**Acceptance Criteria (Story-Level — US-11, Anti-Scam):**

- [ ]  Anti-scam declaration screen appears before the Account Aggregator consent screen for 100% of new users and cannot be dismissed without tapping "I understand" — verified across all onboarding test cases
- [ ]  Declaration reappears on the user’s first login on a new device and after 30 days since the last acknowledgment timestamp
- [ ]  "Report a scam call" is accessible from the Help section in under 3 taps from any screen and opens a pre-filled support contact method

**Modules:**

**M8.1 — Anti-Scam Declaration Module**
Interstitial screen shown before AA consent. Non-dismissible. Bilingual (Hindi/English). “I understand” confirmation tap required. Stores declaration_confirmed_at timestamp in user profile. Re-shows on new device login or after 30-day timeout. User flow: Onboarding → Anti-scam screen → “I understand” tap → AA consent screen.

**M8.2 — Account Aggregator Consent Module**
Displays consent scope: data type, retention period, purpose. Stores consent_given_at, consent_scope, consent_token in encrypted Firestore. Revocation available in Settings → Privacy → Revoke data access. Revocation triggers: AA token invalidation, queued data deletion job (24-hour SLA). User flow: AA consent screen → Scope displayed → [Give Consent] → Token stored → [Settings → Revoke] → Deletion job triggered.

**M8.3 — Data Privacy and Encryption Module**
AES-256 encryption at rest for all Firestore PII fields. TLS 1.2+ for all API calls. Masked Aadhaar storage only (last 4 digits). DPDP Act compliance: privacy notice (Hindi and English) accessible from app footer, data deletion request flow, retention policy (transaction data: 12 months, session data: 30 days). RBAC: users can only access their own data; partner accounts have access to aggregate reports only.

**Figma brand reference:** DhanSathi brand identity file — use brand colours and typography consistently on all trust-layer screens. Midnight Indigo (#1B2F5C) for headers, Cream White (#FDF6ED) for background, Marigold Amber (#E8901A) for the primary CTA button.

https://www.figma.com/design/LdJKi2Tqmgr5gu2SFRjWqM/DhanSathi---Brand-Identity

